00:04:04.145 --> 00:04:08.503
It's as if, again coming from
a place where my identity

00:04:08.503 --> 00:04:11.841
is almost attacked on
TV on a daily basis and

00:04:11.841 --> 00:04:17.065
moving to a place where it's just
celebrated is very refreshing.

00:04:17.065 --> 00:04:21.041
That end of GSB and
also in the campus at large.

00:04:22.332 --> 00:04:25.970
&gt;&gt; yeah thanks, and
building off that point,

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:30.260
can you guys talk about your experiences
specifically with GSB Pride?

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:34.160
Can you talk about some of the social
events that Pride organizes throughout

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.090
the year, any other types of events
that Pride sponsors or organizes?

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.940
So I might throw that one to you,
Duke, and then Jack as well.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.589
&gt;&gt; Sure,
I mean I love the Pride group here,

00:04:44.589 --> 00:04:47.325
I think pride is really
what you make of it.

00:04:47.325 --> 00:04:52.272
So we meet about up once a week
to catch up with each other,

00:04:52.272 --> 00:04:58.471
both classes which as Jack mentioned,
a really valuable thing for me.

00:04:58.471 --> 00:05:04.420
We also do alumni,
we have an alumni reception once a year.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:08.460
We attend rumba,
which is the LGBT conference in the fall.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.170
We do National Coming Out Day.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:16.630
I think we host informal small group
dinners where we get to know one another,

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:18.835
so lots of events throughout the year.

00:05:18.835 --> 00:05:22.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah what I think is really
nice is the touch point of that

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.030
kind of weekly social event that we have.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.990
It's just a nice way of coming together.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.710
You can bring allies, you can bring
friends and people just get together and

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:32.320
sit, and sometimes you talk politics,

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:35.965
much of which I don't understand
as a Brit, I'm trying to avoid it.

00:05:35.965 --> 00:05:36.569
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:39.887
then everything from that,
from things going on in the community, or

00:05:39.887 --> 00:05:42.642
school more broadly, and
it's just a nice space to be able

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:46.202
to get together in a no-pressure
environment once a week, which is nice.

00:05:46.202 --> 00:05:50.877
&gt;&gt; Do either of you have a favorite
GSB pride event from this past year

00:05:50.877 --> 00:05:53.696
that was especially memorable to you?

00:05:53.696 --> 00:05:59.200
&gt;&gt; I think the event was fantastic,
I think you realize that they're not only

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:04.050
rock stars in their lives, but they're
like rock stars in terms of the community,

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:09.090
a lot of them which is fantastic to see
them, and for them to just be so open

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:12.750
to getting to know us, and helping and
supporting I think that was great for me.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:13.543
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
For me,

00:06:13.543 --> 00:06:15.693
I really enjoyed rumba in the fall.

00:06:15.693 --> 00:06:20.033
It was a great first event for entering
students, got to know a bunch of my

00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:24.920
classmates, and met a bunch of new
people from different schools.

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:25.420
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:31.260
So talking a little bit more
about what the GSB life is and

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:36.290
how that intertwines with the GSB Pride
community, can a few of you talk about

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:41.460
your involvement in social and
professional clubs other than GSB Pride?

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:43.560
So Vince you want to talk
about your experiences?

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:47.003
&gt;&gt; Sure, so
I'm a co-president of two clubs currently.

00:06:47.003 --> 00:06:50.629
First is,
I'm a co-president of the game club which

00:06:50.629 --> 00:06:54.498
is basically a bunch of geeks
talking about video games,

00:06:54.498 --> 00:06:59.113
pursuing video as professional career,
so that's pretty great.

00:06:59.113 --> 00:07:04.943
Second is I'm also co-president of
the Asia Business Students Association,

00:07:04.943 --> 00:07:10.424
and what I find is when they find like
coming from Asia and going into foreign

00:07:10.424 --> 00:07:15.992
country in the US, I found I never really
appreciated my identity as an Asian

00:07:15.992 --> 00:07:21.318
until I actually came to a geography
which has a lot of ethnic diversity.

00:07:21.318 --> 00:07:21.841
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:07:21.841 --> 00:07:24.223
&gt;&gt; Yeah and that's,
being part of both GSP Pride and

00:07:24.223 --> 00:07:28.139
the Asian Business Student Association has
helped me appreciate both layers of my

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:28.720
identity.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.508
&gt;&gt; The intersectionality
between those two.

00:07:30.508 --> 00:07:34.635
Yeah, and I guess apart from that, we're
also planning a Philippines Global of

00:07:34.635 --> 00:07:37.075
Study trip, so
hopefully that comes through.

00:07:37.075 --> 00:07:41.478
&gt;&gt; Awesome I guess I can talk to that
as well from my own experiences.

00:07:41.478 --> 00:07:47.032
So aside from being in GSB Pride,
I am also an Arbuckle Leadership Fellow.

00:07:47.032 --> 00:07:51.704
So the Arbuckle Leadership Program
is designed for second-year MBAs and

00:07:51.704 --> 00:07:55.621
help second year MBAs learn how
to develop and coach others and

00:07:55.621 --> 00:07:59.111
be more effective at coaching and
developing others.

00:07:59.111 --> 00:08:02.541
And so we take specific classes
throughout the fall quarter and

00:08:02.541 --> 00:08:07.180
the spring quarter now And in the fourth
quarter when the first year MBAs arrive we

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:12.820
actually take leadership of a squad
of five or six first year MBAs.

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:16.473
And help and develop them to become
better and more effective leaders through

00:08:16.473 --> 00:08:19.296
simulated leadership exercises
throughout the quarter.

00:08:19.296 --> 00:08:22.520
So it's a really awesome experience.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:26.940
I've definitely been learning a lot ever
since joining the program and I feel like

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:30.900
I've been developing my leadership skills
in a new and unique way here at the GSB.

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:36.886
So shifting away from
the pride community and

00:08:36.886 --> 00:08:42.450
extra-curricular activities, I want to
talk a little bit more about career plans.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:47.240
So what are your career plans and
how has your experience at the GSB helped

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.250
shape what you might
want to do in the future?

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:53.820
I might throw that to you,
Jack, to answer that one first.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:58.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I think in terms of
how the GSB has helped shape

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:00.330
how I think about career plans.

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:04.520
I think you're just surrounded by 800,
across the 2 classes,

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:09.630
amazing people who've done a massive
range of interesting things.

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.850
And so I think the first way
it kind of impacted me was it

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:17.030
showed me the art of the possible in
terms of the opportunities out there,

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:20.050
roles I'd never even heard of or
thought about.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:23.580
So I think that was really powerful, just
being able to speak to, and engage with

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:27.570
people, and really dig into what those
kind of opportunities look like.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:32.720
And then I think more broadly
the resources on campus,

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:36.175
things like the career management center,
is a fantastic resource,

00:09:36.175 --> 00:09:39.500
whether that's helping you think
about what you want to do.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:41.482
Which I know I went in with
a blank piece of paper and

00:09:41.482 --> 00:09:43.800
came out with several really good options.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:45.880
And then they help you through
every step of that process,

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:48.390
whether it be interview prep,
CV reviews or

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:54.150
kind of negotiating internship
opportunities and full time roles.

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:56.460
I'm looking to transition
into private equity, and

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:59.480
specifically operating
within private equity.

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:02.383
And lots of our classmates
come from various firms and

00:10:02.383 --> 00:10:04.390
have been really helpful with that.

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:08.690
In particular several members of the pride
community have put me in touch with

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:11.520
their previous employers or people
that they're in conversations with and

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.420
have helped make that connection for
me, yeah.

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:15.750
&gt;&gt; How about you, Duke?

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:18.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah, let me say my career
path has been pretty evolving.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:24.920
I echo Jack that the care services has
been super helpful in thinking about

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:29.410
designing what your career would be and
strategies on how to engage with alums,

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:34.190
and even your Professors on how
to think about your career.

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:37.838
So I came into the GSB thinking
that I wanted to do consulting,

00:10:37.838 --> 00:10:42.539
and I may still do that, but I'm also
exploring tech, specifically to get back

00:10:42.539 --> 00:10:47.110
whether or not, like I would
product management, for example.

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:50.037
And I think that's what's
great about the GSB,

00:10:50.037 --> 00:10:53.322
is you can talk to people who
are experts in the field and

00:10:53.322 --> 00:10:56.907
it really iterate on what you want and
what you're good at.

00:10:56.907 --> 00:10:59.987
&gt;&gt; And I think just to add on that I
have one specific example of kind of

00:10:59.987 --> 00:11:01.109
the community aspect.

00:11:01.109 --> 00:11:04.664
A couple of months back there was
a company on campus, and the day before I

00:11:04.664 --> 00:11:08.061
saw one of our classmates who I had
spoken to about what I wanted to do.

00:11:08.061 --> 00:11:11.980
And he came up to me and was just like
hey, these guys are on campus tomorrow.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:15.620
I've already dropped them an email to
let them know that you're interested and

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:17.080
this is your background.

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:19.610
Because he had made the connection
between what I wanted to do and

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:20.580
what they were talking about.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:23.440
I would've totally missed that
opportunity if that hadn't happened.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:25.500
That was totally proactive on that part,

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:29.180
so I think the community aspect
is really important on that.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:31.170
&gt;&gt; Just to add to the community aspect.

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:34.870
I know I mentioned a while ago that
I'm actually headed back to Bain, but

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:39.050
I think what the GSB has helped me figure
out is what I want to do in the long term,

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:41.390
say five to ten years down the line.

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:44.800
I also mentioned that I'm part of
the game club, and through the game club,

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:48.010
I actually was able to network with
lots of people from the gaming industry.

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:52.210
I met a lot of people from big
companies like Activision Blizzard,

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:53.675
EA Games and what not.

00:11:53.675 --> 00:11:57.810
And really, these conversations with
these different sorts of people who

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:03.220
are passionate about their craft sort
of have inspired me to pursue gaming,

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:04.180
at least this summer.

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:07.650
So this summer, what I'm actually going to
do is I'm going to be working as a product

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:11.480
manager for a midsized gaming
company in San Francisco.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:16.960
I'm working on a video game called
War Dragons, helping create dragons for

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:20.690
people who are going to play that game on
their phone, so that's pretty interesting.

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:21.661
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's awesome.

00:12:21.661 --> 00:12:25.452
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Actually, I want to pick upon
a thing that all of you touched upon,

00:12:25.452 --> 00:12:28.485
which was your interactions
with employers on campus and

00:12:28.485 --> 00:12:32.050
how supportive that the pride
community has been in that.

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:36.720
But talking a little bit more about
your interactions with employers,

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:41.550
can you speak to whether you were out
in your interactions with them, and

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:46.060
generally how receptive have
employers been to your identity?

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:48.630
And if you can speak to experiences now or

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:52.470
experiences before the GSB,
please feel free to.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:55.138
So I might toss that one to you,
Duke, first.

00:12:55.138 --> 00:12:59.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for me I'm pretty
comfortable with being LGBT and

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:02.740
I know not a lot of people are,
so I've always been out at work.

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:05.360
And I personally believe
that it's important for

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:10.963
me to be able to come to work as my
full self, and even on my resume line,

00:13:10.963 --> 00:13:14.600
I think I have a line that says like
enthusiastic RuPaul drag race fan.

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:18.520
[LAUGH] Just to make it clear.

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:21.150
&gt;&gt; How about you, Vince?

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:23.490
&gt;&gt; So I guess for
me it's been a stark contrast.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:28.360
Having worked in oil and gas sales before,
I was actually told by a boss,

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:31.970
very early in my career that I
couldn't out myself to select

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:35.610
clients just because they're really
known to be homophobic and what not.

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:38.810
And my experience in the bay area,
because I've been primarily applying for

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:43.750
internships here, is that being out and
proud is a non factor.

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:48.141
Some people might argue that it might
actually give you some brownie points for

00:13:48.141 --> 00:13:49.880
being a diversity candidate.

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:54.984
So if anything, being out and proud
while applying to different companies,

00:13:54.984 --> 00:13:57.600
as a GSB person, is a good thing.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.620
&gt;&gt; Good,
do you have anything to add, Jack?

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:04.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think certainly, a lot of the
big employers are really good in engaging

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:08.870
with the community and helping with
that kind of process of applying.

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:12.423
And I think one of the things
that I noticed is that,

00:14:12.423 --> 00:14:17.880
especially if you're talking to alumni or
companies with alumni about them,

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:22.364
that you come with this brand
that's already really powerful.

00:14:22.364 --> 00:14:26.722
And actually they know so much about you,
and they've been part of the GSB

00:14:26.722 --> 00:14:31.595
community before, so I think it's been
really well received in general, yeah.

00:14:31.595 --> 00:14:33.605
&gt;&gt; All right, thanks for
your perspectives on that one.

00:14:33.605 --> 00:14:37.880
I'm actually seeing that a bunch of
questions are coming in from the audience,

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:42.300
so I want to have some time to
answer some of those as well.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:48.600
So the first question that I'm seeing
is hi team, thank you for time today.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:49.369
You're welcome.

00:14:49.369 --> 00:14:54.260
[LAUGH] I was wondering what is
the women to men ratio in the GSB Pride?

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:58.090
Just asking,
since you're all men in the panel today.

00:14:58.090 --> 00:15:00.260
So that's a great point.

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:05.210
We do have a number of women in
GSB Pride who are super super active in

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.820
organizing a lot of
the activities that we do.

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:12.160
Just to give an example,
of the four presidents this past year,

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:16.490
three of them identified as women,
Alice, Jess, and Ellen.

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:20.606
We have a number of events that
occur throughout the year,

00:15:20.606 --> 00:15:25.231
one of them most recently happened
in San Francisco, I believe.

00:15:25.231 --> 00:15:29.420
It was a brunch that was attended
by over 25 plus GSB Pride women and

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:31.960
alumni from around the bay area.

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:34.000
So there's a super,
super active community.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:38.020
I can't give exact numbers on
the ratio and percentage right now,

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:40.050
because I don't know them
off the top of my head.

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:44.970
But they certainly are a big part of our
community and an important one as well.

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:51.860
And then another question that
we got was when applying,

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:54.060
were you out on your application?

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:57.280
Could you discuss how you communicated it?

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:59.290
Does anybody want to take one of those?

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:00.290
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I can take it.

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:03.210
I was definitely out on my application.

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:08.970
Mostly because I had talked about it in
my essay and what you know coming out.

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:12.700
And being from my family's
pretty Catholic and

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:15.740
how that shaped me into
the person that I am today.

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:19.580
And I was involved in a bunch
of LGBT organizations too.

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:22.715
So, that was my [INAUDIBLE].

00:16:22.715 --> 00:16:26.540
&gt;&gt; I did the exact thing too.

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:30.720
In my what matters most and
why I say talk about equality and

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:34.930
I think of course lots of elements of
equality that I was able to weave in were

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:39.820
from you know me being queer and
in Southeast Asia so

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:43.100
meeting out came out there and of course
your CV can communicate it as well.

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:45.300
&gt;&gt; How about you, Jack?

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:47.440
Were you on your application?

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:48.300
&gt;&gt; I don't think I was.

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:51.460
I think it's just, if it feels.

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:54.480
My thoughts on this all you have to
be authentic in your application and

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:58.280
if it authentically fits into
your story in your essay or

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:03.080
in your in other parts of your
application then certainly flag it.

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:08.290
Yeah, I think it's more about being
authentic to answering the questions.

00:17:08.290 --> 00:17:09.630
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that's a great point.

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:15.130
And I've gotten this question a couple
times as well from perspective admits our

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:20.710
or my advice every time
has been if being LGBT and

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:26.060
if that part of your identity is
a huge part of who you are today,

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:30.090
what makes you a great leader, and what
makes you a great fit for the GSB, then

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:34.780
I would absolutely put it in your essays
or in other parts of your application.

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:39.420
What I wouldn't recommend is
putting it in just to put it in,

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:41.880
without any particular reason.

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:46.400
Because that's something that admission
definitely tries to tease out is who you

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:52.750
are as person and what kind of
value are you at the GSP community.

00:17:53.800 --> 00:18:00.360
Other questions from the audience,
how active are the allies when it comes to

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:05.680
defending someone who identifies
as LGBTQ in a birth situation.

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:08.790
Do you have any interesting
situations to share?

00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:12.800
I'll give you guys a couple
moments to think about that.

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:19.660
Or maybe to the first question,
how supportive have you perceived

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:25.910
allies to be on campus and would they come
to defend you in interesting situations?

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:29.100
&gt;&gt; I think, so
I think more broadly at the GSB,

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:32.030
I think there's very
rarely a situation where

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:36.170
I could see that happening in in any
form of diversity or any situation.

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:39.070
I don't think I've seen
an argument since being here,

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:42.850
unless it's a heated debate in in
a case or in in the classroom.

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:47.340
And I'd certainly see very often
people voicing opinions of those

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:52.551
are simple when discussing cases or or
just talking diversity more broadly,

00:18:52.551 --> 00:18:55.998
a sense of allyship and
then people would step in and

00:18:55.998 --> 00:19:00.190
certainly are involved in
the community just more probably.

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:05.171
&gt;&gt; Anyone else have any situations?

00:19:05.171 --> 00:19:08.430
Like really, there's no situations that-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:11.250
I guess it's just not an issue.

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:14.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that's the general
theme that I'm hearing from

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:18.730
all of you is that it doesn't really
seem to be an issue on campus.

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:21.300
That said,
we do benefit from being in the Bay Area,

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:22.640
we do benefit from being in California.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:27.560
So we can't speak to all
environments everywhere.

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:33.350
But certainly at Stanford in
Palo Alto specifically there,

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:37.500
I've been out and pretty young,
I haven't experienced any issues.

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:39.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; I was just saying,

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:40.560
I think it's a healthy discussion.

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:43.710
I think one of the things that
happens a lot at the GSBs is

00:19:43.710 --> 00:19:46.620
the healthy discussion around
a collection of topics.

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:50.010
Whether that be your identity,
whether that be diversity or

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:54.340
politics or career or so I think I've
been in a lot of situations that people

00:19:54.340 --> 00:19:57.430
are certainly tested and challenged and
asked questions and thought about things.

00:19:57.430 --> 00:19:59.320
And so
I think you see that happening a lot and

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:01.980
I think that's a healthy
debate that we see.

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:03.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah absolutely.

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:06.950
And I think on the allyship point
we have tremendous allies who

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:10.960
come to our Pride events all the time and
I know next year Eddy is planning on,

00:20:10.960 --> 00:20:14.740
Doing more formal engagements or

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:19.160
socials with the different diversity clubs
out there with women in management group.

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:27.030
Yeah, do you want to
talk a bit about that?

00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:27.950
&gt;&gt; Yes, so I think.

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:30.020
I'm sorry I had to think about
what you were referring to.

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:32.870
Yeah so we just got elected.

00:20:32.870 --> 00:20:35.390
We just chose our new leadership
just a couple weeks ago.

00:20:35.390 --> 00:20:38.470
So we've just been in the process of
putting together our agenda for next year.

00:20:38.470 --> 00:20:44.080
And one of the things that we're really
focusing on, really organizing and

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:47.610
adding into the agenda,
are more community-focused initiatives and

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:50.380
specifically education-focused
initiatives.

00:20:50.380 --> 00:20:54.900
Not just for GSB Pride but for
the broader Stanford and GSB community.

00:20:54.900 --> 00:21:00.480
I think if there's one thing that we would
wish to do more as GSB Pride members is,

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:04.390
have the ability to have
open conversations with

00:21:04.390 --> 00:21:09.450
other students within the GSB community.

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:12.890
We do receive a lot of questions about
how people can be a better ally.

00:21:12.890 --> 00:21:17.830
So because of this interest,
we've just decided to have more

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:22.910
events around you know, just like casual
lunches but also formal asked me anything

00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:26.320
type of events which we plan
on organizing for next year.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:27.850
So that's all in the pipeline and

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:31.280
we're really kind of excited to kick,
kick off, kick all that off.

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:37.190
So I want to switch gears a little bit to
another audience question that was more

00:21:37.190 --> 00:21:43.430
around the lines of how we chose to
come to Stanford versus other programs.

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:46.310
And what research we did to
really inform that twice.

00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:51.530
So one question was,
''during the research phase of your MBA

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:57.130
application journey What questions
Did you ask alumni the GSB?

00:21:57.130 --> 00:22:01.270
Or did you ask in general,
about other programs?

00:22:01.270 --> 00:22:06.250
And how did that inform your decision to
choose Stanford over another program,

00:22:06.250 --> 00:22:08.500
whether it was in the country or
internationally.

00:22:09.560 --> 00:22:10.850
I might throw this one to you,

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:13.770
Jack because I know personally
you're making this choice.

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:17.800
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I probably did it in
a slightly unconventional way.

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:21.770
I definitely didn't plan
to do business school, and

00:22:21.770 --> 00:22:26.625
so kind of a few months before round one I

00:22:26.625 --> 00:22:31.955
downloaded the FT's top ten school list,
and kind of started from there.

00:22:31.955 --> 00:22:36.455
And outside of the kind of year-round
sunshine that Stanford provides,

00:22:36.455 --> 00:22:40.417
which was really important to me,
I think I thought about

00:22:41.437 --> 00:22:44.317
what it was that I wanted to get
in terms of skills development.

00:22:44.317 --> 00:22:48.247
And then I thought about how I'd
like to develop those skills.

00:22:48.247 --> 00:22:51.297
And for me, it was a lot about
how is the course structured.

00:22:51.297 --> 00:22:53.347
What flexibility do I have in that?

00:22:53.347 --> 00:22:55.937
And then,
what's the community going to be like?

00:22:55.937 --> 00:23:01.120
And I think for me that turning point
was admit weekend when I kind of

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:05.120
was looking at various options and
decided to come to on that weekend and

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:10.110
then I just had this weekend everybody
on that as well you are my people and so

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:13.610
I think that's a really important
factor that is hard to test for.

00:23:13.610 --> 00:23:18.450
Through, you know, websites or
statistics, I think, for me,

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:22.440
it's what I want to spend two years
with and who do I want to go with.

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:23.535
So that was really important.

00:23:23.535 --> 00:23:25.310
Yeah.

00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:28.860
&gt;&gt; Do any of you have similar experiences?

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:32.380
&gt;&gt; I applied to, so
my application story was I,

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:37.050
that I just applied to the top three
schools, I got in a couple of them, and

00:23:37.050 --> 00:23:41.890
ultimately the choice boiled
down to where my partner and

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:46.900
I could, my partner and I intended to move
to another country where we could get

00:23:46.900 --> 00:23:53.150
married or have kids, and it seemed that
Stanford provided the perfect environment,

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:57.870
being in the Bay Area to make
the transition easy for us,

00:23:57.870 --> 00:24:03.150
because whenever we have to fly back to
Manila, which is just a direct flight

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:09.010
from San Fransisco,
that flight will be easily available.

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:13.509
And to San Francisco is known
as the game mecca, so to speak.

00:24:13.509 --> 00:24:19.074
So that is like that just makes
the whole transition less easier for us.

00:24:19.074 --> 00:24:21.438
&gt;&gt; I can talk about my
own experience as well.

00:24:21.438 --> 00:24:26.465
So for me I was really torn
in trying to decide between

00:24:26.465 --> 00:24:31.391
Stanford or
an international school like INSEAD.

00:24:31.391 --> 00:24:34.754
One of the benefits of going to
an international school is that you have

00:24:34.754 --> 00:24:37.612
a little bit more of an international
focus which is great.

00:24:37.612 --> 00:24:42.818
But at the end of the day what it
boils down to me was where did I feel

00:24:42.818 --> 00:24:47.738
like I could develop most as a leader,
and having a two-year

00:24:47.738 --> 00:24:51.201
program at Stanford to me kind of-
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]

00:24:51.201 --> 00:24:53.252
&gt;&gt; A one year program at an international

00:24:53.252 --> 00:24:54.604
school somewhere else.

00:24:54.604 --> 00:24:59.579
So that's how I made my choice,
even though my fiancee is French and

00:24:59.579 --> 00:25:02.387
definitely wanted me to go to INSEAD.

00:25:02.387 --> 00:25:06.885
Had to let them down.

00:25:06.885 --> 00:25:12.795
[LAUGH] So
switching gears now into Mentorship,

00:25:12.795 --> 00:25:17.226
one question from the audience is,

00:25:17.226 --> 00:25:24.328
how do you personally engage with mentors,
GSB alumni?

00:25:24.328 --> 00:25:29.999
I know we talked about the alumni event,
but are there other avenues that any

00:25:29.999 --> 00:25:36.485
of you have experienced with engaging with
any mentors or alumni through the network?

00:25:36.485 --> 00:25:41.414
&gt;&gt; I think one of the things that you
get at the GSB is lots of moments

00:25:41.414 --> 00:25:45.799
of mentorship that might not
specifically be a mentor.

00:25:45.799 --> 00:25:50.974
So I was telling the guys just before
I came in that I did a small group

00:25:50.974 --> 00:25:56.906
dinner with a faculty member here who is
a very successful VC in the Bay Area.

00:25:56.906 --> 00:26:01.849
And at that small group dinner offered up
just kind of one on one conversations.

00:26:01.849 --> 00:26:05.609
And two, three weeks later
I'm start having coffee for

00:26:05.609 --> 00:26:09.614
30 minutes talking about
anything that was on my agenda.

00:26:09.614 --> 00:26:13.362
Whether that was whether I wanted to
pitch a startup idea or whether I wanted

00:26:13.362 --> 00:26:16.703
to talk about my career, or
how to manage the world that is the GSB.

00:26:16.703 --> 00:26:21.304
That's one example of where that
mentorship happens in the moment.

00:26:21.304 --> 00:26:26.415
I know I think my favorite class of the
first quarter is managerial skills where

00:26:26.415 --> 00:26:31.447
basically, you get these rock stars who
come in and teach you basically how to

00:26:31.447 --> 00:26:36.420
hire people, how to fire people,
how to manage difficult conversations.

00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:39.680
And again we did this small group
dinner with our faculty member there,

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:41.147
we had follow-up coffees, and

00:26:41.147 --> 00:26:44.803
these are people that you couldn't get
time with if you tried in the real world.

00:26:44.803 --> 00:26:49.378
And so I think you get lots of moments of
small mentorship, which I think you can

00:26:49.378 --> 00:26:52.927
grow then into longer term
relationships if it feels right.

00:26:52.927 --> 00:26:56.223
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
I've had a similar experience as well,

00:26:56.223 --> 00:27:00.372
when I attended the reaching out
MBA conference this past fall.

00:27:00.372 --> 00:27:06.272
I had met a GSB Pride alumni
there who I didn't know before at

00:27:06.272 --> 00:27:12.413
a reception at Google, and
him and I connected immediately.

00:27:12.413 --> 00:27:17.102
After the reception, he was absolutely
instrumental in helping me through

00:27:17.102 --> 00:27:21.654
the process and helping me with
interviews, helping me with questions.

00:27:21.654 --> 00:27:25.581
So I really, really felt like he,
he wanted me to succeed.

00:27:25.581 --> 00:27:29.393
And I think without that GSB pride
connection, I'm not sure if I would have

00:27:29.393 --> 00:27:32.722
been able to connect with any
other recruiter at the conference,

00:27:32.722 --> 00:27:33.647
because of that.

00:27:33.647 --> 00:27:34.979
So that's just one example.

00:27:34.979 --> 00:27:39.979
I mean I think the punch line here
is like we found the alumni really,

00:27:39.979 --> 00:27:46.057
really supportive even if we only see them
a couple times per year at ad hoc events.

00:27:46.057 --> 00:27:47.260
They're always there for support.

00:27:47.260 --> 00:27:50.469
&gt;&gt; I guess we also haven't touched
on is MBA too, as mentors, so

00:27:50.469 --> 00:27:54.300
during your first year you take this
class called Leadership Labs in which

00:27:54.300 --> 00:27:56.989
you're assigned an Arbuckle
leadership fellow.

00:27:56.989 --> 00:28:01.261
They'll lead you along with a group of six
in leadership development exercises where

00:28:01.261 --> 00:28:03.646
you pick up different soft skills and
what not.

00:28:03.646 --> 00:28:06.291
And I found that to be
a very good mentorship tool.

00:28:06.291 --> 00:28:09.816
And in the two succeeding quarters
you can actually get paired

00:28:09.816 --> 00:28:14.358
with a personal leadership coach in
the form of an Arbuckle Leadership Fellow,

00:28:14.358 --> 00:28:16.186
who will meet up with you seven or

00:28:16.186 --> 00:28:19.676
eight times to fulfill whatever
leadership needs you have.

00:28:19.676 --> 00:28:24.577
And for me, that was very powerful
because in those specific moments,

00:28:24.577 --> 00:28:29.640
I had particular conversations I had
to have with regard to say, trying to

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:35.296
push a job offer, or for example, extend
the deadline of a job offer and whatnot.

00:28:35.296 --> 00:28:40.374
So that time with that coach as
very invaluable, and yeah, what I'm

00:28:40.374 --> 00:28:46.171
trying to say is MBA 2 is, can serve as
mentors, it serves as mentors as well.

00:28:46.171 --> 00:28:50.340
And I guess just to allude to what Jack
said, I learned so much from MBA 1 through

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:54.701
our season, and experienced different
sorts of environments and industries that

00:28:54.701 --> 00:28:59.274
I may treat them as mentors for particular
things in other times, for certain topics.

00:28:59.274 --> 00:29:00.823
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:29:00.823 --> 00:29:03.545
On that topic of leadership actually,

00:29:03.545 --> 00:29:08.247
another question submitted by
the audience is what are you learning

00:29:08.247 --> 00:29:12.798
about leadership while at the GSB
that you didn't already know?

00:29:12.798 --> 00:29:15.733
And this is a really,
really important question so

00:29:15.733 --> 00:29:18.677
I'd like for, I guess for
everybody to answer it.

00:29:18.677 --> 00:29:19.658
Do you want to start?

00:29:19.658 --> 00:29:23.821
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean I think one thing
that I've learned is the power of

00:29:23.821 --> 00:29:28.381
vulnerability in the context of
management that it helps build trust

00:29:28.381 --> 00:29:33.352
that it helps folks connect with you
that you are managing or working with.

00:29:33.352 --> 00:29:35.984
I think that came as a surprise for me.

00:29:35.984 --> 00:29:40.389
Because I'm not naturally vulnerable.

00:29:40.389 --> 00:29:44.896
But I think there are tons of
opportunities at the GSP to practice that

00:29:44.896 --> 00:29:45.526
muscle.

00:29:45.526 --> 00:29:48.521
And really build that trust
with your classmates.

00:29:48.521 --> 00:29:51.062
&gt;&gt; Actually vulnerability was
the first answer that came to my head.

00:29:51.062 --> 00:29:53.150
But because Duke took it-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:29:53.150 --> 00:29:55.236
&gt;&gt; I guess not leadership in particular,

00:29:55.236 --> 00:29:57.202
but developing leadership skills.

00:29:57.202 --> 00:30:01.726
I think what I learned that was
very valuable at the GSB was

00:30:01.726 --> 00:30:04.836
immerse yourself in situations that,

00:30:04.836 --> 00:30:09.937
like What came to mind was the value
of role playing and what not.

00:30:09.937 --> 00:30:14.523
Basically, what I'm leading
to is just keep immersing

00:30:14.523 --> 00:30:19.208
yourself in situations that
you think you may experience

00:30:19.208 --> 00:30:23.711
in the future that you feel
like getting what you like.

00:30:23.711 --> 00:30:28.100
Immerse yourself in situations
that you feel will help develop

00:30:28.100 --> 00:30:30.842
your leadership skills in the future.

00:30:30.842 --> 00:30:31.756
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So, say for

00:30:31.756 --> 00:30:35.734
example in that Arbuckle Leadership class
we would role play certain things like

00:30:35.734 --> 00:30:37.850
firing an employee, hiring an employee.

00:30:37.850 --> 00:30:40.307
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; Having a difficult conversation with

00:30:40.307 --> 00:30:44.833
someone like two layers above, and just
emerging yourself in those situations that

00:30:44.833 --> 00:30:49.440
you could potentially face in the future,
like will end up developing those skills.

00:30:49.440 --> 00:30:52.032
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
I think one thing you hear a lot here is

00:30:52.032 --> 00:30:56.856
that leadership is a set of behaviors
rather than something that is intrinsic to

00:30:56.856 --> 00:30:58.086
a specific person.

00:30:58.086 --> 00:31:02.772
So I think to build on what you were
saying, that if you've put yourself into

00:31:02.772 --> 00:31:06.508
situations where you are able
to build on those behaviors and

00:31:06.508 --> 00:31:10.337
exercise those behaviors,
you will become a better leader.

00:31:10.337 --> 00:31:11.107
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:31:11.107 --> 00:31:12.159
How about you, Jack?

00:31:12.159 --> 00:31:17.541
&gt;&gt; I think for me, leadership comes in so
many forms and it's very personal.

00:31:17.541 --> 00:31:22.260
I think one of the things we talked about
is leadership [INAUDIBLE] program and

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:27.290
basically, the ultimate question is why
would somebody follow you as a leader?

00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:32.153
And at the end of the course, you
basically go around as a team and say why

00:31:32.153 --> 00:31:37.766
you would or wouldn't follow everybody as
a leader, and no one answers the same.

00:31:37.766 --> 00:31:42.329
And I think everybody, there is a reason
that everybody can be a leader and

00:31:42.329 --> 00:31:46.671
there is these things that make them
really impactful as a leader, and

00:31:46.671 --> 00:31:48.819
that might be a variety of things.

00:31:48.819 --> 00:31:53.017
So you can take a set of tools, a set of
behaviors, make it authentic to you, and

00:31:53.017 --> 00:31:55.417
that's what's going to
make you successful.

00:31:55.417 --> 00:31:59.638
If you try and play a playbook,
it's not going to work.

00:31:59.638 --> 00:32:02.586
And I think that's what's nice is
really getting the feedback and

00:32:02.586 --> 00:32:06.060
understanding like why people follow you
and what behaviors work for you, and

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:08.604
also seeing the different
types of leadership play out.

00:32:08.604 --> 00:32:12.099
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and just to add on to what
everybody said, one of the first things

00:32:12.099 --> 00:32:16.590
that I thought about during that question
is vulnerability and openness as well.

00:32:16.590 --> 00:32:21.700
Which I think just speaks to how
Stanford has hit this concept home for

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:24.423
everybody, on a repeated basis, and

00:32:24.423 --> 00:32:29.890
just to provide an example of how
that's incorporated in our curriculum.

00:32:29.890 --> 00:32:33.588
One of the classes that I'm taking right
now is called Interpersonal Dynamics,

00:32:33.588 --> 00:32:35.630
a.k.a, touchy feely,

00:32:35.630 --> 00:32:40.960
and it's really one of the cornerstone
classes of the GSB experience.

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:46.290
And it’s essentially a class in which
you learn all the different models and

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:49.860
concepts on how to understand how
to communicate with others and

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:54.300
why breakdowns in communications happen,
why conflicts occur between people.

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:59.190
And part of the class is
literally sitting in a room

00:32:59.190 --> 00:33:02.265
with 13 other people in a circle for

00:33:02.265 --> 00:33:07.510
three hours at a time, and
just letting the communication unfold.

00:33:07.510 --> 00:33:10.620
And it sounds super ambiguous and
unstructured,

00:33:10.620 --> 00:33:15.250
but I've had two classes so
far and it's been absolutely

00:33:17.430 --> 00:33:21.500
riveting to just sit in this group and

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:27.180
to see how communication unfolds and
taking risks and

00:33:27.180 --> 00:33:33.530
expressing how you actually feel is such
an out of the comfort zone for me, but I'm

00:33:33.530 --> 00:33:37.340
kind of understanding now how that would
make me a better leader in the future.

00:33:38.630 --> 00:33:42.850
So a lot of things that were unexpected
for me coming into the GSB about

00:33:42.850 --> 00:33:48.800
leadership, but
I'm definitely learning a lot.

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:52.070
So, and we have about ten minutes or
so left.

00:33:52.070 --> 00:33:54.090
So I think we can answer
a few other questions,

00:33:56.220 --> 00:34:00.840
there's a question from
the audience around how being

00:34:00.840 --> 00:34:05.580
LGBTQ has informed how you show up and
be present in the classroom.

00:34:06.760 --> 00:34:11.200
So does anyone have any thoughts about if
you made a contribution that was informed

00:34:11.200 --> 00:34:12.620
by your identity?

00:34:12.620 --> 00:34:17.130
Or whether you viewed
something a different way or

00:34:17.130 --> 00:34:20.000
brought a new perspective
because you were LGBTQ?

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:23.540
Any experiences like that in
the classroom or in group meetings?

00:34:25.020 --> 00:34:27.410
&gt;&gt; I guess not in particular
in the classroom but

00:34:27.410 --> 00:34:30.070
I guess one thing we
can talk about is talk.

00:34:30.070 --> 00:34:34.400
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So talk is like a weekly event in which

00:34:34.400 --> 00:34:37.810
we get two of our classmates to speak
in front of the whole student body and

00:34:37.810 --> 00:34:41.560
share maybe for 30 to 45 minutes
just their life story and whatnot.

00:34:41.560 --> 00:34:43.085
Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And I guess so

00:34:43.085 --> 00:34:47.245
I had my talk last quarter and
year three has ended, thank you.

00:34:47.245 --> 00:34:47.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] It was great.

00:34:47.910 --> 00:34:49.340
&gt;&gt; It was fantastic, yeah.

00:34:49.340 --> 00:34:53.390
&gt;&gt; So I had my talk last quarter, and that
just gave me a venue to tell people about

00:34:53.390 --> 00:34:57.535
my identity and my journey to the GSB and
my journey in life thus far.

00:34:57.535 --> 00:35:01.005
And a lot of that involved my
identity as a queer person.

00:35:01.005 --> 00:35:03.405
And I feel like that just
served as a venue for

00:35:03.405 --> 00:35:06.075
me to share my life story to everyone.

00:35:06.075 --> 00:35:08.485
And hopefully people, queer and

00:35:08.485 --> 00:35:12.385
not queer were able to just
take something from that.

00:35:12.385 --> 00:35:12.885
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:35:14.120 --> 00:35:18.630
&gt;&gt; I think for me something that I think
about is sort of coming into school I

00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:22.614
very much played a role at work
during turn around and crisis and

00:35:22.614 --> 00:35:28.570
it was very much relatively straight faced
and I was there to do a specific thing.

00:35:28.570 --> 00:35:31.806
And I think I came into the classroom
a little bit like that,

00:35:31.806 --> 00:35:34.786
thinking this is business,
this is business school,

00:35:34.786 --> 00:35:38.380
let's talk about whatever
the case is in front of us.

00:35:38.380 --> 00:35:41.210
And then what I've seen is all my
classmates bringing in this wider

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:45.250
perspective around who they are as
a person and their identity and

00:35:45.250 --> 00:35:48.370
bringing that to the conversations
that we're having, and

00:35:48.370 --> 00:35:49.980
I've seen how powerful that's been.

00:35:49.980 --> 00:35:54.901
And so I think less and less am I
disconnecting who I am and what I'm doing

00:35:54.901 --> 00:35:59.905
and bringing more of myself to those
conversations into the classroom so

00:35:59.905 --> 00:36:05.236
I think for me it's still a process I'm
going through, but I have definitely

00:36:05.236 --> 00:36:10.540
seen how it's been impactful in
the conversation that we've been having.

00:36:10.540 --> 00:36:14.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I can just talk from my,
I did not personally do this but

00:36:14.360 --> 00:36:18.063
I know of someone who made

00:36:18.063 --> 00:36:23.620
an informed comment in class where
it was our marketplaces class and

00:36:23.620 --> 00:36:29.050
we were talking about dating apps
as a form of marketplaces and

00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:34.530
someone that I know actually
raised their hand and

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:37.200
pointed out to the professor
in the classroom that

00:36:37.200 --> 00:36:40.700
we're looking at dating apps from
a very heteronormative perspective and

00:36:40.700 --> 00:36:44.990
we should also consider
the implications of a marketplace for

00:36:46.970 --> 00:36:53.340
a queer dating app, or like,
Tinder, or same-sex dating app.

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:56.530
So it was a really insightful,
I found that to be a super,

00:36:56.530 --> 00:36:57.770
super insightful comment.

00:36:57.770 --> 00:37:00.400
And to be a very brave comment, as well.

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:06.050
But I could literally see people's
faces changing in the room from,

00:37:06.050 --> 00:37:11.360
to just being curious, but
to just a broader understanding of,

00:37:11.360 --> 00:37:14.239
these are issues that we actually need to
think about and we need to be aware of.

00:37:15.580 --> 00:37:17.260
When talking about the real world.

00:37:17.260 --> 00:37:21.040
So I thought that was a really, really
cool experience that I witnessed recently.

00:37:27.136 --> 00:37:30.678
I think another question, this one is
a little bit more about social life.

00:37:30.678 --> 00:37:35.340
At the GSB-LGBTQ night life at the GSB so
we can answer this one

00:37:35.340 --> 00:37:38.680
really quickly because I know I was
curious, at least, when I was playing.

00:37:40.040 --> 00:37:43.190
How is LGBTQ night life near the GSB?

00:37:43.190 --> 00:37:45.480
What do we do?

00:37:46.640 --> 00:37:52.710
Are there venues, do we organize things on
campus or do we go out to San Francisco?

00:37:52.710 --> 00:37:55.085
Kind of leading you guys
to that answer right now.

00:37:55.085 --> 00:37:56.840
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I think one of the fantastic things

00:37:56.840 --> 00:38:02.710
about business school,
whether it be pride specific or

00:38:02.710 --> 00:38:07.960
non, if you got 50 really interesting
people in a room, you can make a party.

00:38:07.960 --> 00:38:09.360
That's really easy to do.

00:38:09.360 --> 00:38:14.260
So is Palo Alto the best night
life in the world, absolutely not.

00:38:14.260 --> 00:38:17.260
Do you need it to be, absolutely not.

00:38:17.260 --> 00:38:20.490
We have our weekly PGPG,
which is our kind of happy hour for

00:38:20.490 --> 00:38:24.600
pride, which we really make
the most of and enjoy.

00:38:24.600 --> 00:38:29.250
And then we head up to the city for
various events, or just to go and

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:32.110
enjoy the night life every
few weeks probably I think.

00:38:32.110 --> 00:38:36.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think we go up to the city
pretty often, one or twice a month.

00:38:36.330 --> 00:38:40.975
There's a good chunk of
your social life is also

00:38:40.975 --> 00:38:44.640
traveling with your classmates over
the weekend, so I would say that

00:38:44.640 --> 00:38:48.600
that has been the constraining factor in
terms of how often we go up to the city.

00:38:48.600 --> 00:38:53.050
So not necessarily like
that Palo Alto was bad.

00:38:53.050 --> 00:38:55.580
Which it is but
there's other stuff out there.

00:38:58.130 --> 00:39:01.130
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would say, so
one question that I had that I know

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:06.355
that some others might have is
how accessible is San Francisco?

00:39:06.355 --> 00:39:09.850
Cause Vince you mentioned that
San Francisco is a very welcoming city to

00:39:10.920 --> 00:39:14.810
people who identify as LGBTQ,
and it absolutely is.

00:39:14.810 --> 00:39:18.940
San Francisco is super,
super accessible from Palo Alto.

00:39:18.940 --> 00:39:21.760
You can either take
a public transportation,

00:39:21.760 --> 00:39:25.900
like a train there, and it gets you there
in like, correct me if I'm wrong, like?

00:39:25.900 --> 00:39:26.420
&gt;&gt; 35 minutes.

00:39:26.420 --> 00:39:30.230
&gt;&gt; 35 minutes to an hour or
if it's at night and

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:33.010
you want to go to experience
some of the night life.

00:39:33.010 --> 00:39:37.740
I know that we kind of just pile into
an Uber and split the Uber four ways and

00:39:37.740 --> 00:39:41.650
it winds up being really
economical to get there and back.

00:39:41.650 --> 00:39:45.920
&gt;&gt; Sometimes we take a bus, so we've done
events as well where we've gone with other

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:49.700
schools and other grads kind of
students and we've gone up and

00:39:49.700 --> 00:39:52.268
done kind of bigger overnight trips
up to the city and back as well.

00:39:52.268 --> 00:39:55.870
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for sure.

00:39:55.870 --> 00:40:01.548
So we have five minutes left, so
we want to end on one question and

00:40:01.548 --> 00:40:08.720
that's, what do you find most rewarding so
far about the Stanford experience?

00:40:11.113 --> 00:40:12.140
Jack, do you want to go?

00:40:12.140 --> 00:40:15.780
&gt;&gt; Yeah, my answer is a compound answer.

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:17.470
It's really easy, it's the people.

00:40:17.470 --> 00:40:18.519
&gt;&gt; Yeah, [LAUGH].

00:40:18.519 --> 00:40:23.379
&gt;&gt; And
I think You're basically surrounded by 420

00:40:23.379 --> 00:40:28.525
classmates who have your back,
are really, really interesting,

00:40:28.525 --> 00:40:33.868
care deeply about one or
many things that they're passionate about.

00:40:33.868 --> 00:40:38.216
And so I think the most rewarding thing
is how my mind has been opened up to so

00:40:38.216 --> 00:40:41.882
many opportunities and so
many interesting conversations.

00:40:41.882 --> 00:40:43.711
And how those friendships and

00:40:43.711 --> 00:40:46.713
relationships are only
going to continue to grow,

00:40:46.713 --> 00:40:51.414
whether that be from a professional
perspective or personal perspective.

00:40:51.414 --> 00:40:55.777
I think the most rewarding part is getting
to do it with this group of people.

00:40:58.380 --> 00:40:59.661
Especially these guys.

00:40:59.661 --> 00:41:02.718
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I guess apart from yeah,

00:41:02.718 --> 00:41:04.728
Jack put it very well.

00:41:04.728 --> 00:41:06.845
My first answer would
definitely be the people,

00:41:06.845 --> 00:41:09.697
the network you could get out of
the business school experience.

00:41:09.697 --> 00:41:14.604
But for me personally I think what
I gained from the GSB most thus

00:41:14.604 --> 00:41:19.617
far is just being able to explore
gaming as a long-term career.

00:41:19.617 --> 00:41:25.104
Because having had multiple conversations
with different people about gaming,

00:41:25.104 --> 00:41:26.933
potentially interning for

00:41:26.933 --> 00:41:31.228
a gaming company this summer
really solidified my hypothesis or

00:41:31.228 --> 00:41:35.857
my thesis of trying to test whether
I want to do gaming in the long term.

00:41:35.857 --> 00:41:38.565
And I feel that if I
hadn't gone to the GSB,

00:41:38.565 --> 00:41:42.264
I wouldn't even be trying to
pursue this thing long term.

00:41:42.264 --> 00:41:46.735
So that may have potentially dictated what
I'm going to do for the rest of my life,

00:41:46.735 --> 00:41:48.977
and I think that's very valuable.

00:41:48.977 --> 00:41:53.008
And another thing we were talking about is
the opportunity to take Tyra Banks' class

00:41:53.008 --> 00:41:53.595
in spring.

00:41:53.595 --> 00:41:55.917
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; She’s lurking around campus right now

00:41:55.917 --> 00:41:56.863
as we do this right now.

00:41:56.863 --> 00:41:57.375
&gt;&gt; She is on campus for that.

00:41:57.375 --> 00:42:00.868
&gt;&gt; Some people saw her
30 minutes ago [LAUGH].

00:42:00.868 --> 00:42:03.624
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:42:03.624 --> 00:42:08.463
I would also echo that people
are absolutely critical, and

00:42:08.463 --> 00:42:15.844
I have met tremendous people here who are
friendly, caring, interesting, super nice.

00:42:15.844 --> 00:42:18.863
So I would say that is top.

00:42:18.863 --> 00:42:22.342
I think for me also time is a huge thing,

00:42:22.342 --> 00:42:26.677
in terms of you are super
busy at the MBA program.

00:42:26.677 --> 00:42:32.208
But you have a lot of time for
self reflection, for career exploration,

00:42:32.208 --> 00:42:36.029
to learn about anything you want,
essentially.

00:42:36.029 --> 00:42:40.726
What's great about the GSB is you can
also take classes outside the GSB at

00:42:40.726 --> 00:42:42.740
other schools than Stanford.

00:42:42.740 --> 00:42:47.332
So it's basically a two-year
opportunity to meet great people,

00:42:47.332 --> 00:42:49.710
learn super interesting things,

00:42:49.710 --> 00:42:53.903
and just reflect on who you are as
a person and what you value.

00:42:53.903 --> 00:42:57.247
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and just to wrap up for
the group before we go,

00:42:57.247 --> 00:43:01.427
I think the most rewarding part
about the Stanford experience for

00:43:01.427 --> 00:43:05.762
me is having an opportunity to take
risks in a low risk environment.

00:43:05.762 --> 00:43:10.492
So before coming to the GSB,
I considered myself a really,

00:43:10.492 --> 00:43:17.028
really risk averse person, both in the
workplace, but also in my personal life.

00:43:17.028 --> 00:43:21.360
And just we've only been at the GSB for
what, like seven months now?

00:43:21.360 --> 00:43:23.417
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And it's completely changed my

00:43:23.417 --> 00:43:26.206
perspective on what it
means to take risks.

00:43:26.206 --> 00:43:32.554
So I'm doing activities that I would never
have done before outside of the GSB,

00:43:32.554 --> 00:43:37.781
like I'm taking a swim class when
I had no idea how to swim before.

00:43:37.781 --> 00:43:40.794
I'm testing new ways to express myself.

00:43:40.794 --> 00:43:45.269
I'm testing new ways to communicate
with others almost on a daily basis.

00:43:45.269 --> 00:43:51.037
And it's really, really exciting and scary
at times, but it's all for the learning.

00:43:51.037 --> 00:43:56.645
And I can't stress this enough that being
at Stanford is a low risk environment,

00:43:56.645 --> 00:44:01.771
and they really cultivate you to learn
how to be a person that takes risks.

00:44:01.771 --> 00:44:09.584
And that's been by far and away the
biggest benefit, so far, in my first year.

00:44:09.584 --> 00:44:12.640
So we'll see what's in store for
us in our second year.

00:44:12.640 --> 00:44:15.318
But I'm really,
really excited for that and

00:44:15.318 --> 00:44:20.056
sad as well that we're almost halfway
through our experience [LAUGH] together.

00:44:20.056 --> 00:44:26.012
But anyway, I think that's all
the time that we have today.

00:44:26.012 --> 00:44:29.960
So thank you to the three of you for
coming and participating.

00:44:29.960 --> 00:44:34.504
Thank you everyone in the audience for
tuning in to the live webcast.

00:44:34.504 --> 00:44:39.335
You can learn more about
the MBA program by visiting

00:44:39.335 --> 00:44:43.276
the GSB website at gsb.stanford.edu.

00:44:43.276 --> 00:44:48.637
And I think that's it for us.

00:44:48.637 --> 00:44:53.403
Also, if you have questions
about the Pride group,

00:44:53.403 --> 00:44:59.919
feel free to email
gsb_pride-leadership@stanford.edu as well.

00:44:59.919 --> 00:45:01.829
But thank you again, and have a great day.

00:45:01.829 --> 00:45:07.632
[MUSIC]

